Solid design overall, but there's a real bug and a couple of inconsistencies worth flagging.

## Bug: `insert` won't compile

```cpp
Node** cur = &(*&root_);  // pointer to unique_ptr slot
```

`&root_` is `std::unique_ptr<Node>*`, so `&(*&root_)` is also `std::unique_ptr<Node>*`. You're assigning that to `Node**`, which is a type mismatch. The variable `cur` is also never read; only `slot` is used. Just delete the line:

```cpp
bool insert(T value) {
    std::unique_ptr<Node>* slot = &root_;
    while (*slot) {
        Node* n = slot->get();
        if (value < n->value)        slot = &n->left;
        else if (n->value < value)   slot = &n->right;
        else                          return false;  // duplicate
    }
    *slot = std::make_unique<Node>(std::move(value));
    ++size_;
    return true;
}
```

## Inconsistency: recursive traversal undoes the stack-safety of `clear()`

You went out of your way to make destruction iterative for degenerate trees, but `inOrder` (and therefore `toSortedVector`) is recursive. A skewed tree of N nodes will still blow the stack on traversal. If stack-safety is a stated design goal, traversal should match:

```cpp
void inOrderTraversal(const std::function<void(const T&)>& visit) const {
    std::vector<const Node*> stack;
    const Node* cur = root_.get();
    while (cur || !stack.empty()) {
        while (cur) { stack.push_back(cur); cur = cur->left.get(); }
        cur = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();
        visit(cur->value);
        cur = cur->right.get();
    }
}
```

## Smaller things

- **`std::function` in a hot path.** For a header-only template, take the visitor as a template parameter to avoid the type-erasure overhead and enable inlining:
  ```cpp
  template <typename F>
  void inOrderTraversal(F visit) const { ... }
  ```
- **Comparator.** Mirroring `std::set`, add a `Compare = std::less<T>` template parameter so the tree works with types that don't have `operator<`, or with custom orderings.
- **`contains` is limiting.** A `find` returning `const T*` (or an iterator, if you add one) is more useful — callers can read the stored value, which matters when `T` has equivalent-but-not-identical instances (e.g., a struct keyed on one field).
- **`empty()` vs `size_`.** You return `!root_`; using `size_ == 0` is equivalent and keeps the two state variables consistent in your head. Minor.
- **`reserve(size_)` in `toSortedVector`** is good. Note it relies on `size_` staying accurate, which it does — just worth a comment that it's the source of truth.
- **Extensions list.** For `erase`, the tricky part with `unique_ptr` is the two-child case: you typically swap values with the in-order successor and then erase the successor's slot, since you can't easily "splice" `unique_ptr` ownership without moving the value. Worth noting because people new to `unique_ptr` trees often try to rewire pointers and fight the ownership model.

